---
// "The ABCD of Data in Manufacturing" — a talk for first-year students.
// Article + presentable slides, using the AIMM template colours.
// Dotted-underlined terms show a plain-language definition on hover or tap.
import '../../slides/shared/deck.css';
import '../../slides/aimm/aimm.css';
import '../../slides/abcd/abcd.css';
import qrRaw from '../../slides/abcd/qr-abcd.svg?raw';

const headline = 'The ABCD of Data in Manufacturing';
const title = `${headline}: a beginner’s guide — Nitish Singh`;
const description =
  'A beginner-friendly guide for engineering students: how factories turn data into decisions with AI, Business Intelligence, Continuous improvement and Data infrastructure. Article and interactive slides.';
const canonical = new URL('/slides/abcd-of-data/', Astro.site);
const ogImage = new URL('/assets/images/og/abcd-of-data.png', Astro.site);
const qr = qrRaw
  .replace(/<\?xml[^>]*\?>/, '')
  .replace(/width="[^"]*"\s+height="[^"]*"/, 'width="100%" height="100%"');
const MIT = 'https://cisr.mit.edu/publication/2024_1201_EnterpriseAIMaturityModel_WeillWoernerSebastian';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" class="article">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <meta name="author" content="Nitish Singh" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="article:author" content="Nitish Singh" />
    <meta property="article:published_time" content="2026-11-18" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={headline} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={ogImage} />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={headline} />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content={ogImage} />
    <link rel="shortcut icon" href="/assets/images/SiteLogo.png" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#a31642" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'BlogPosting',
      headline,
      description,
      datePublished: '2026-11-18',
      author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Nitish Singh', url: 'https://nitishsingh.com' },
      image: ogImage.toString(),
      mainEntityOfPage: canonical.toString(),
      audience: { '@type': 'EducationalAudience', educationalRole: 'student' },
    })} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-bar">
      <a class="brand" href="/"><b>NS</b><span>Nitish Singh</span></a>
      <a href="/projects/">Projects</a>
      <a href="/talks/">Talks</a>
      <button type="button" data-deck="theme" aria-label="Toggle light or dark theme">◐</button>
    </header>

    <main class="article-wrap">
      <div class="art-head">
        <div class="art-kicker">Talk for students · article + live slides</div>
        <h1>{headline}</h1>
        <p class="art-dek">
          How factories turn data into decisions — explained for first-year students, with
          interactive slides. Words with a <span class="term" data-t="kpi">dotted underline</span>
          explain themselves when you hover or tap them.
        </p>
        <div class="byline"><b>Nitish Singh</b><span>November 2026</span><span>10 min read</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="viewer">
      <div class="deck-shell" id="deck-shell" tabindex="0" aria-label="Slides: The ABCD of Data in Manufacturing">
        <div class="stage" id="stage" data-footer="The ABCD of Data in Manufacturing">

          <!-- 1 · Cover -->
          <section class="slide cover" id="b-title" data-title="Title">
            <div class="cover-top">
              <div class="cover-kicker">Guest lecture · first-year students</div>
              <div class="abcd-tiles"><span>A</span><span>B</span><span>C</span><span>D</span></div>
              <h1>The ABCD of Data in Manufacturing</h1>
            </div>
            <div class="cover-band">
              <svg class="blobs" viewBox="0 0 1600 432" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice" aria-hidden="true">
                <g fill="none" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.09)" stroke-width="150" stroke-linecap="round">
                  <path d="M80 520 C 80 300, 360 300, 360 120" />
                  <path d="M720 -60 C 720 200, 1020 200, 1020 420" />
                  <path d="M1250 520 C 1250 330, 1520 330, 1520 140" />
                </g>
              </svg>
              <p class="sub">Artificial intelligence · Business intelligence · Continuous improvement · Data infrastructure — how factories turn data into decisions</p>
              <div class="cover-meta"><span>Nitish Singh, PhD</span><span>Data Scientist, KMWE · Assistant Professor, TU/e</span></div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>Tip for students: any word with a dotted orange underline shows a short explanation when you hover or tap it.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 2 · About me -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-me" data-title="About me">
            <div class="kicker">Background</div>
            <h2>From mechanical engineering to factory data</h2>
            <div class="grow center-v"><div class="journey">
              <div class="jn"><i>⚙️</i><b>B.Tech. Mechanical Engineering</b><span>VIT University, India</span><small>2010 – 2014</small></div>
              <div class="jn"><i>🌍</i><b>Senior Project Consultant</b><span>Schneider Electric — India, Brazil, China</span><small>2014 – 2017</small></div>
              <div class="jn"><i>🎓</i><b>MSc &amp; PhD</b><span>TU Eindhoven</span><small>2017 – 2025</small></div>
              <div class="jn now"><i>🏭</i><b>Data Scientist</b><span>KMWE, Brainport Industries Campus</span><small>2024 – present</small></div>
              <div class="jn now"><i>🧑‍🏫</i><b>Assistant Professor (part-time)</b><span>TU Eindhoven</span><small>2025 – present</small></div>
            </div></div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>Point out that you started where they are now: a mechanical-engineering degree, not a data degree.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 3 · Why it matters -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-why" data-title="Why this matters">
            <div class="kicker">Why this matters</div>
            <h2>We need to do more with less</h2>
            <div class="row grow" style="gap: 40px; align-items: center;">
              <div class="col" style="flex: 1.15; gap: 18px;">
                <div class="card num-card"><b>1</b><div><h3>Products keep getting more complex</h3><p>A bicycle has a few hundred parts; a modern aircraft has millions. Making them reliably means tracking far more information.</p></div></div>
                <div class="card num-card"><b>2</b><div><h3>Automation keeps growing</h3><p>Robots and autonomous systems take on harder tasks, so planning and controlling them matters more than ever.</p></div></div>
                <div class="card num-card"><b>3</b><div><h3>The digital shop floor</h3><p>Machines and processes get a digital representation, tied together by shared standards such as <span class="term" data-t="isa95">ISA-95</span> and <span class="term" data-t="rami">RAMI 4.0</span> — and <span class="term" data-t="agent">AI agents</span> are starting to change how work flows.</p></div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="card" style="flex: 0.85; text-align: center;">
                <div class="stat-big">2.1M</div>
                <p style="color: var(--text) !important; font-weight: 700;">manufacturing jobs in the US could go unfilled by 2030</p>
                <p class="src">Deloitte &amp; The Manufacturing Institute, 2021</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>The jobs figure is from a widely cited 2021 US study by Deloitte and The Manufacturing Institute; the point is the trend — fewer people, harder products.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 4 · Firefighting -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-fires" data-title="Factories today">
            <div class="kicker">Manufacturing today</div>
            <h2>Too often, factories are just fighting fires <span class="fire">🔥 firefighting</span></h2>
            <div class="grid-2 grow" style="align-content: center;">
              <div class="card num-card"><b>1</b><div><h3>Escalation-driven</h3><p>Every line and every shift brings new problems, with little time to solve them structurally.</p></div></div>
              <div class="card num-card"><b>2</b><div><h3>Time drain</h3><p>People on the shop floor spend much of their time searching for information and doing admin, not solving problems.</p></div></div>
              <div class="card num-card"><b>3</b><div><h3>Knowledge loss</h3><p><span class="term" data-t="tribal">Tribal knowledge</span> walks out of the door with every technician who retires.</p></div></div>
              <div class="card num-card"><b>4</b><div><h3>Talent gap</h3><p>Shortages of skilled people are structural, and growing.</p></div></div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>Ask the students: what does “firefighting” mean in a factory? Reacting to problems instead of preventing them.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 5 · The problem -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-problem" data-title="The problem">
            <div class="kicker">The problem</div>
            <h2>Data is everywhere. Insight is scarce.</h2>
            <div class="grow center-v">
            <div class="row" style="gap: 40px; align-items: center;">
              <div style="flex: 1.2;">
                <p class="lead">Factories create huge amounts of data every day: <span class="term" data-t="cnc">CNC machines</span>, <span class="term" data-t="erp">ERP</span> systems, quality logs and <span class="term" data-t="sensor">sensors</span>. Yet only a small part of it is used to make decisions.</p>
                <div class="grid-3" style="margin-top: 28px;">
                  <div class="card"><h3>Siloed systems</h3><p><span class="term" data-t="erp">ERP</span>, <span class="term" data-t="mes">MES</span> and <span class="term" data-t="plm">PLM</span> don’t talk to each other — <span class="term" data-t="silo">data silos</span>.</p></div>
                  <div class="card"><h3>Manual reporting</h3><p>Hours spent copying data into Excel instead of acting on it.</p></div>
                  <div class="card"><h3>No feedback loop</h3><p>Insights never make it back to the shop floor.</p></div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="card" style="flex: 0.55; text-align: center;">
                <div class="stat-big">~20%</div>
                <p style="color: var(--text) !important; font-weight: 700;">of industrial data is used for decisions</p>
                <p class="src">An often-quoted industry estimate; the exact number varies by study.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>Be careful with the 20% figure — it is a rough, widely repeated estimate, not a precise measurement. The message is that most data is never used.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 6 · Data to action -->
          <section class="slide demo" id="b-pipe" data-title="From data to action">
            <div class="kicker">The goal · click each step</div>
            <h2>The right data, at the right time, to the right person</h2>
            <div class="grow center-v">
            <div id="pp-row" class="pp-row"></div>
            <div id="pp-detail" class="card pp-detail"></div>
            <div class="ladder">
              <div><b>Descriptive</b>What happened?</div>
              <div><b>Diagnostic</b>Why did it happen?</div>
              <div><b>Predictive</b>What will happen?</div>
              <div><b>Prescriptive</b>What should we do?</div>
            </div>
            <p class="tap-hint" style="margin: 10px 0 0;">The aim is to climb this <span class="term" data-t="descriptive">analytics ladder</span>, from describing the past to recommending actions.</p>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>Data → information → action. Walk through the steps with the machine example. Then the ladder: most factories are on the first rung.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 7 · ABCD overview -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-flywheel" data-title="The ABCD flywheel">
            <div class="kicker">The ABCD</div>
            <h2>Four letters that work together</h2>
            <div class="row grow" style="gap: 60px; align-items: center;">
              <div class="fw">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 560 560" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="280" cy="280" r="200" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="6" stroke-dasharray="14 12" /></svg>
                <div class="fw-node fw-a"><div><b>A</b><span>AI</span></div></div>
                <div class="fw-node fw-b"><div><b>B</b><span>BI</span></div></div>
                <div class="fw-node fw-c"><div><b>C</b><span>CI</span></div></div>
                <div class="fw-node fw-d"><div><b>D</b><span>DI</span></div></div>
                <div class="fw-mid">ABCD<br />flywheel</div>
              </div>
              <div class="letters" style="flex: 1;">
                <div class="letter-row"><b class="bg-A">A</b><div><h3><span class="term" data-t="ai">Artificial intelligence</span></h3><p>finds patterns in the data</p></div></div>
                <div class="letter-row"><b class="bg-B">B</b><div><h3><span class="term" data-t="bi">Business intelligence</span></h3><p>makes them visible to people</p></div></div>
                <div class="letter-row"><b class="bg-C">C</b><div><h3><span class="term" data-t="ci">Continuous improvement</span> culture</h3><p>acts on the insights, every day</p></div></div>
                <div class="letter-row"><b class="bg-D">D</b><div><h3>Data infrastructure</h3><p>makes all of it possible</p></div></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>A flywheel: each turn makes the next one easier. We’ll go through A, B, C, D — but in practice you build D first.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 8 · Divider A -->
          <section class="slide divider" id="b-div-a" data-title="A — Artificial intelligence">
            <div class="dv-letter">A</div>
            <div class="dv-k">Part A</div>
            <h2>Artificial intelligence</h2>
            <p>Practical AI for the shop floor — not the hype</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 9 · What AI means -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-ai" data-title="What AI means in manufacturing">
            <div class="kicker">A · Artificial intelligence</div>
            <h2>AI has a lot of promise — but work comes first</h2>
            <div class="grow center-v">
            <div class="row" style="gap: 36px; align-items: flex-start;">
              <div style="flex: 1;">
                <p style="color: var(--text) !important;">AI in manufacturing is not about replacing people. It adds pattern recognition at scale to people’s expertise: <span class="term" data-t="ml">machine-learning</span> models trained on <b>your</b> process data, guided by <b>your</b> engineering knowledge, solving <b>your</b> problems.</p>
                <h3 style="margin-top: 18px;">Before any “intelligence”, three roles do the groundwork</h3>
                <div class="roles3">
                  <div><b>Data engineer</b>gets the data flowing and clean</div>
                  <div><b>Data analyst</b>turns it into answers</div>
                  <div><b>Business user</b>knows the real problem</div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="nt" style="flex: 1;">
                <div class="hd">Not this</div><div class="hd">This</div>
                <div class="no">Generic chatbot</div><div class="yes">Process-specific <span class="term" data-t="anomaly">anomaly detection</span></div>
                <div class="no">Black-box predictions</div><div class="yes">Explainable settings optimisation</div>
                <div class="no">Needs big data teams</div><div class="yes">Starts with one use case</div>
                <div class="no">Replaces operators</div><div class="yes">Gives operators better insight</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>Ground expectations: most of the work in an AI project is getting the data right.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 10 · AI maturity + use cases -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-ai2" data-title="AI maturity and use cases">
            <div class="kicker">A · Artificial intelligence</div>
            <h2>Most companies are still early on the AI journey</h2>
            <div class="grow center-v">
            <div class="mat">
              <div><b>28%</b><h4>1 · Experiment &amp; prepare</h4><p>Educating people, setting AI policies, making data accessible.</p></div>
              <div><b>34%</b><h4>2 · Build pilots</h4><p>Simplifying processes, creating use cases, sharing data through interfaces.</p></div>
              <div><b>31%</b><h4>3 · Develop AI ways of working</h4><p>Scaling up, building for reuse, exploring autonomous <span class="term" data-t="agent">agents</span>.</p></div>
              <div><b>7%</b><h4>4 · AI future-ready</h4><p>AI built into decisions and processes; new AI-enabled services.</p></div>
            </div>
            <p class="src" style="margin: 8px 0 18px;">Share of enterprises per stage — MIT CISR Enterprise AI Maturity Model (Weill, Woerner &amp; Sebastian, 2024)</p>
            <h3>Examples of classical and modern AI in a factory</h3>
            <div class="uc">
              <div><b><span class="term" data-t="anomaly">Anomaly detection</span></b>Flag a machine whose sensor data drifts from its normal behaviour.</div>
              <div><b><span class="term" data-t="twin">Digital twin</span></b>Simulate the shop floor with live data to test “what if?” plans.</div>
              <div><b>AI assistant</b>A chatbot that helps <span class="term" data-t="cam">CAM</span> engineers, using the factory’s own data.</div>
              <div><b>Agents + tools</b><span class="term" data-t="agent">Agents</span> that reach data through <span class="term" data-t="mcp">MCP</span> and <span class="term" data-t="kg">knowledge graphs</span>.</div>
            </div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>The MIT CISR model is based on a survey of enterprises; only 7% were at stage 4.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 11 · Divider B -->
          <section class="slide divider d-B" id="b-div-b" data-title="B — Business intelligence">
            <div class="dv-letter">B</div>
            <div class="dv-k">Part B</div>
            <h2>Business intelligence</h2>
            <p>The nervous system — translating operations into decisions</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 12 · BI + OEE -->
          <section class="slide demo" id="b-oee" data-title="KPIs and OEE">
            <div class="kicker">B · Business intelligence · try it</div>
            <h2>One source of truth, and the most famous factory KPI</h2>
            <div class="grow center-v">
            <p style="margin: 0 0 16px; color: var(--text) !important;">No more downloading data into Excel and spending hours on reports: everyone looks at the same <span class="term" data-t="kpi">KPIs</span> on a <span class="term" data-t="dashboard">dashboard</span>. The best-known one is <span class="term" data-t="oee">OEE</span>. Move the sliders for one 8-hour shift:</p>
            <div class="oc">
              <div class="card oc-ctl">
                <label for="oc-down">Machine stopped (breakdowns, waiting) <span id="oc-down-v"></span></label>
                <input id="oc-down" class="range" type="range" min="0" max="240" step="5" value="60" />
                <label for="oc-speed">Running slower than ideal <span id="oc-speed-v"></span></label>
                <input id="oc-speed" class="range" type="range" min="0" max="40" value="10" />
                <label for="oc-scrap">Parts that fail quality checks <span id="oc-scrap-v"></span></label>
                <input id="oc-scrap" class="range" type="range" min="0" max="20" value="3" />
                <p class="oc-note">Assumes 480 planned minutes and an ideal speed of one part per minute.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="card oc-out">
                <div class="oc-big"><b id="oc-oee">—</b><span>OEE = Availability × Performance × Quality</span></div>
                <div class="oc-bar"><span>Availability</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl fl-a" id="oc-a"></div></div><b id="oc-a-n"></b></div>
                <div class="oc-bar"><span>Performance</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl fl-p" id="oc-p"></div></div><b id="oc-p-n"></b></div>
                <div class="oc-bar"><span>Quality</span><div class="tr"><div class="fl fl-q" id="oc-q"></div></div><b id="oc-q-n"></b></div>
                <div class="oc-note" id="oc-parts"></div>
                <div class="oc-hint" id="oc-hint"></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>Ask the room to guess a typical OEE before moving the sliders. The 85% “world-class” figure is often quoted, but many plants are well below it.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 13 · Drill-down -->
          <section class="slide demo" id="b-drill" data-title="From top floor to shop floor">
            <div class="kicker">B · Business intelligence · try it</div>
            <h2>Same data, different views</h2>
            <div class="row" style="gap: 12px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 12px;">
              <span class="ctl-label" style="margin: 0;">I am the…</span>
              <div class="seg-control" id="dr-role">
                <button type="button" data-role="ceo">CEO</button>
                <button type="button" data-role="planner">Planner</button>
                <button type="button" data-role="operator">Operator</button>
              </div>
              <span class="tap-hint" style="margin-left: auto;">Fictional example</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dr grow" id="dr">
              <div class="dr-lv" id="dr-l1">
                <div class="dr-tag">Level 1 · Top floor</div>
                <div class="dr-kpi"><b><span class="term" data-t="otd">OTD</span> 91.3%</b><span>On-time delivery · target 95% · 3 <span class="term" data-t="wo">work orders</span> at risk this week</span><button type="button" class="btn small primary" id="dr-why">Why? Drill down ↓</button></div>
              </div>
              <div class="dr-lv" id="dr-l2">
                <div class="dr-tag">Level 2 · Work orders at risk — click one</div>
                <div class="dr-wos" id="dr-wos"></div>
              </div>
              <div class="dr-lv" id="dr-l3">
                <div class="dr-tag">Level 3 · Shop floor — daily stand-up board</div>
                <table class="dr-board" id="dr-board"></table>
              </div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>BI closes the loop: the CEO sees OTD %, the planner sees the work orders at risk, the operator sees today’s priority list — all from the same data.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 14 · KPI frameworks -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-kpis" data-title="KPI frameworks">
            <div class="kicker">B · Business intelligence</div>
            <h2>Don’t reinvent the wheel — use proven KPI frameworks</h2>
            <div class="fwk grow" style="align-content: center;">
              <div class="card">
                <h3><span class="term" data-t="iso22400">ISO 22400</span></h3>
                <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--text) !important;">KPIs for manufacturing operations management</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Standard definitions for KPIs such as OEE, throughput, quality rate and setup ratio</li>
                  <li>The same calculation across plants and shifts</li>
                  <li>Easy to compare with other companies</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="card">
                <h3><span class="term" data-t="scor">SCOR</span> by ASCM</h3>
                <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--text) !important;">Supply Chain Operations Reference model</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>KPIs for the whole supply chain: reliability, responsiveness, agility, cost, assets</li>
                  <li>Links shop-floor numbers to what the customer experiences</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="scor"><span>Plan</span><span>Source</span><span>Make</span><span>Deliver</span><span>Return</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>Standards save endless arguments about how to calculate a number.</p></aside>
          </section>

          <!-- 15 · Divider C -->
          <section class="slide divider d-C" id="b-div-c" data-title="C — Continuous improvement culture">
            <div class="dv-letter">C</div>
            <div class="dv-k">Part C</div>
            <h2>Continuous improvement culture</h2>
            <p>Culture beats strategy every time</p>
          </section>

          <!-- 16 · Culture -->
          <section class="slide" id="b-culture" data-title="Data culture">
            <div class="kicker">C · Continuous improvement culture</div>
            <h2>Technology is only part of the equation</h2>
            <div class="kill-enable grow">
              <div class="card kill">
                <h3>What kills a data culture</h3>
                <ul>
                  <li>“We’ve always done it this way”</li>
                  <li>Departments keeping data to themselves</li>
                  <li>Fear of transparency and accountability</li>
                  <li>Orders from the top without shop-floor buy-in</li>
                  <li>Blaming people instead of fixing processes</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="card enable">
                <h3>What enables it</h3>
                <ul>
                  <li>Operators own their own data and dashboards</li>
                  <li>Celebrate small wins publicly</li>
                  <li>Show data on the shop floor, not only in offices</li>
                  <li>Train <span class="term" data-t="literacy">data literacy</span>, not just tools</li>
                  <li>Leaders use data in daily stand-ups</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 16px;"><b>Key insight:</b> many digital transformations fail, and people and culture are the reason more often than technology. Start with people, not platforms.</div>
            <aside class="notes"><p>You may have heard “70% of digital transformations fail” — it is a popular estimate. The exact number matters less than the cause: resistance to change, lack of vision, weak foundations.</p></aside>
          </section>

