      <article class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          When I give this talk to first-year students, I start with a confession: I studied
          mechanical engineering, not data science. Today I work with factory data every day, as a
          data scientist in a precision-manufacturing company and as an assistant professor at TU
          Eindhoven. This article is the written version of that talk.
        </p>
        <p>
          You don't need any background in data to follow it. Words with a
          <span class="term" data-t="kpi">dotted underline</span> show a short explanation when you
          hover over them or tap them, and there's a full glossary at the end.
        </p>

        <h2>Why factories need data now</h2>
        <p>
          Products have become far more complex — a bicycle has a few hundred parts, a modern
          aircraft has millions. At the same time there are fewer skilled people to make them: one
          widely cited US study (Deloitte and The Manufacturing Institute, 2021) estimated that 2.1
          million manufacturing jobs could go unfilled by 2030. Factories have to do more with less.
        </p>
        <p>
          In practice, many factories spend their days firefighting: every shift brings new
          problems, people spend a lot of time searching for information, and
          <span class="term" data-t="tribal">tribal knowledge</span> disappears when experienced
          colleagues retire. Meanwhile, machines, <span class="term" data-t="erp">ERP</span>
          systems, quality logs and <span class="term" data-t="sensor">sensors</span> produce huge
          amounts of data. Only a small part of it — an often-quoted estimate is about 20% — is used
          to make decisions, because systems are <span class="term" data-t="silo">silos</span>,
          reports are made by hand, and insights never reach the shop floor.
        </p>

        <h2>The goal: from data to action</h2>
        <p>
          The goal of a data strategy fits in one sentence: get the right data to the right person at
          the right time, so they can make better decisions. That happens in six steps: connect the
          sources, collect and translate the data, store it in one place, analyze it, act on it, and
          improve. Along the way you climb the
          <span class="term" data-t="descriptive">analytics ladder</span>, from “what happened?” to
          “what should we do?”.
        </p>
        <p class="try">Click through the six steps, with a machine example, on <a href="#6" data-slide="6">slide 6</a>.</p>
        <p>
          I organise everything around four letters that reinforce each other like a flywheel:
          <strong>A</strong>rtificial intelligence finds patterns, <strong>B</strong>usiness
          intelligence makes them visible, a <strong>C</strong>ontinuous improvement culture acts on
          them, and <strong>D</strong>ata infrastructure makes it all possible.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="letter-h bg-A">A</span>Artificial intelligence</h2>
        <p>
          <span class="term" data-t="ai">AI</span> in a factory is not about replacing people. It
          adds pattern recognition at scale to people's expertise:
          <span class="term" data-t="ml">machine-learning</span> models trained on your own process
          data and guided by your own engineering knowledge. Think
          <span class="term" data-t="anomaly">anomaly detection</span> on a specific machine rather
          than a generic chatbot, and explainable recommendations rather than black boxes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The catch is that a lot of work comes first. A data engineer has to get the data flowing,
          an analyst has to turn it into answers, and a business user has to know which problem is
          worth solving. That's why most companies are still early on: in MIT CISR's
          <a href={MIT} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Enterprise AI Maturity Model</a>, only 7% of
          the enterprises surveyed had reached the most advanced stage. Newer ideas such as
          <span class="term" data-t="agent">AI agents</span> that use
          <span class="term" data-t="mcp">MCP</span> and
          <span class="term" data-t="kg">knowledge graphs</span> make this foundation even more
          important, not less.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="letter-h bg-B">B</span>Business intelligence</h2>
        <p>
          <span class="term" data-t="bi">Business intelligence</span> turns data into reports and
          <span class="term" data-t="dashboard">dashboards</span>, so that everyone looks at the same
          numbers instead of their own spreadsheet. Those numbers are
          <span class="term" data-t="kpi">KPIs</span>. The most famous one in manufacturing is
          <span class="term" data-t="oee">OEE</span>: availability (was the machine running?) times
          performance (at full speed?) times quality (making good parts?).
        </p>
        <p class="try">Play with an OEE calculator on <a href="#12" data-slide="12">slide 12</a> — you'll see how quickly small losses multiply.</p>
        <p>
          Good BI also links the top floor to the shop floor. The CEO sees on-time delivery
          (<span class="term" data-t="otd">OTD</span>); the planner drills down to the
          <span class="term" data-t="wo">work orders</span> at risk; the operator sees today's
          priority list. Same data, different views. And you don't have to invent your own KPIs:
          standards such as <span class="term" data-t="iso22400">ISO 22400</span> and the
          <span class="term" data-t="scor">SCOR</span> model already define them.
        </p>
        <p class="try">Try the drill-down as CEO, planner and operator on <a href="#13" data-slide="13">slide 13</a>.</p>

        <h2><span class="letter-h bg-C">C</span>Continuous improvement culture</h2>
        <p>
          Culture beats strategy. Many digital transformations fail, and the reason is more often
          people than technology. A data culture dies when departments keep data to themselves,
          when people fear transparency, or when changes are pushed from the top without shop-floor
          buy-in. It grows when operators own their own dashboards, small wins are celebrated,
          data is visible on the shop floor, people learn
          <span class="term" data-t="literacy">data literacy</span>, and leaders use data in their
          daily stand-ups. That's what <span class="term" data-t="ci">continuous improvement</span>
          looks like with data.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="letter-h bg-D">D</span>Data infrastructure</h2>
        <p>
          Infrastructure isn't glamorous, but everything rests on it. Factories are organised in
          levels — the automation pyramid from <span class="term" data-t="isa95">ISA-95</span>:
          <span class="term" data-t="sensor">sensors</span> and actuators at the bottom, then
          <span class="term" data-t="plc">PLCs</span>, <span class="term" data-t="scada">SCADA</span>,
          <span class="term" data-t="mes">MES</span> and <span class="term" data-t="erp">ERP</span> at
          the top. Decisions at the top take months; at the bottom, milliseconds.
        </p>
        <p class="try">Click through the pyramid on <a href="#18" data-slide="18">slide 18</a>.</p>
        <p>
          The shop floor (<span class="term" data-t="itot">OT</span>) and the office
          (<span class="term" data-t="itot">IT</span>) speak different languages, so you need a
          <span class="term" data-t="semantic">semantic layer</span> to translate between them. Most
          companies grow step by step from spreadsheets to a
          <span class="term" data-t="db">database</span>, a
          <span class="term" data-t="dwh">data warehouse</span>, a
          <span class="term" data-t="lakehouse">lakehouse</span> and finally
          <span class="term" data-t="streaming">streaming</span> data. You don't have to jump to the
          end: start where you are. If you want to go deeper, I've written about the
          <a href="/slides/uns/">Unified Namespace</a> and about
          <a href="/slides/data-platform/">data platforms</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>What this looks like in a real factory</h2>
        <p>
          In a precision-machining factory, these ideas turn into small pilot projects with one
          clear goal each: measuring when machines actually run, tracking work orders, simulating the
          shop floor with a <span class="term" data-t="twin">digital twin</span>, steering on the top
          reasons for downtime, <span class="term" data-t="pdm">predictive maintenance</span>, and an
          AI assistant for <span class="term" data-t="cam">CAM</span> engineers. Notice that almost
          every one starts with D: getting the data first.
        </p>

        <h2>Where to start</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>D — fix the foundation first.</strong> Pick one data silo, connect it to a central database, and prove it works.</li>
          <li><strong>B — build one dashboard</strong> that people recognise and use.</li>
          <li><strong>A — pilot one AI use case</strong>, small and measurable.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          And <strong>C</strong>, continuous improvement, from the very first day. Test yourself
          with the quiz on <a href="#22" data-slide="22">slide 22</a>: is it A, B, C or D?
        </p>

        <h2>Glossary</h2>
        <dl class="glossary" id="gl-article"></dl>

        <p class="note">
          The examples in the slides are simplified or fictional. Statistics marked as estimates are
          widely quoted figures, not precise measurements.
        </p>
      </article>
